  "response": "Because socialization-based and ability-or-preference-based accounts of women's underrepresentation in science and engineering generate overlapping predictions about who enrolls where, adjudication cannot proceed from enrollment counts and must instead seek the points at which the accounts come apart. The common ground is itself informative: at least one of the following holds, and perhaps both do\u2014differential socialization of girls and boys contributes to the gender gap in STEM participation, and differences in occupational preferences between women and men contribute to that gap.\n\nThe first strategy is to test auxiliary predictions experimentally. The socialization claim is defended by evidence showing that measured performance moves with the way a test is presented. Spencer, Steele, and Quinn found that framing a difficult math test as diagnostic of ability depressed women's performance relative to men's. The greater male variability hypothesis predicts a higher ratio of males to females at the extreme upper tail of the mathematics ability distribution. The greater male variability hypothesis predicts equal numbers of men and women in the top one percent of mathematics performance, and that prediction cannot be squared with the skewed upper-tail ratio, since one and the same tail cannot be male-weighted and evenly divided.\n\nBy contrast with that prediction about the extreme upper tail of the ability distribution, the sharpest leverage has come from cross-national comparison, which asks whether gaps shrink where social conditions for women improve. Stoet and Geary reported that countries ranking higher on gender-equality indices had lower shares of women among STEM graduates. Guiso and colleagues found that cross-country measures of gender equality were associated with smaller gender gaps in PISA mathematics scores. Guiso and colleagues reported that the mathematics gender gap was of identical magnitude in every country they examined, a report that cannot be squared with the association they documented between national equality measures and the size of that gap, since a gap that varies with equality is not a gap of uniform size.\n\nA third route measures attitudes directly. Nosek and colleagues found that nation-level implicit stereotypes linking science with males predicted national sex differences in eighth-grade science achievement, and this result confirms the inference drawn from cross-national variation in the size of the STEM gender gap, which is cited as evidence that social environments shape participation. The literature on evaluation and hiring supplies a fourth. Moss-Racusin and colleagues found that science faculty rated identical application materials bearing a male name as more competent than those bearing a female name. Ceci and Williams argued that occupational preferences rather than hiring discrimination account for women's scarcity in math-intensive academic fields. Set against that preference-based conclusion is the distribution of degrees in the life sciences, which admits of only two possibilities: either women earn the majority of bachelor's degrees awarded in the biological sciences in the United States, a majority that an account resting on preference would not lead one to expect, or women earn fewer than half of all bachelor's degrees awarded in the biological sciences in the United States; one of these must be the case, and not both. Adjudication thus turns on such divergent auxiliary predictions rather than on the enrollment patterns the competing accounts share.",
